That’s a great example of organized visual chaos. I’d call it Pokémon Maximalist Camo or, more technically, Figurative Dazzle Camouflage.

What makes it interesting is that it’s simultaneously a collage, a pattern, and a kind of camouflage.

It’s doing several things at once:

* Maximalist collage: Hundreds of distinct characters packed into one visual field.
* Character camouflage: Individual Pokémon become difficult to distinguish because their outlines overlap and compete.
* Visual saturation: Nearly every available space contains information, leaving very little negative space.
* Semantic camouflage: You recognize Pokémon immediately, but identifying any particular Pokémon takes focused attention.
* Multiscale patterning: From a distance, it reads as colorful texture. Up close, it resolves into individual characters and stories.

What I’d name this family of patterns

Name	Emphasis
Semantic Camo	Meaningful objects concealed among other meaningful objects
Maximalist Mosaic	Dense, interconnected imagery
Recognition Camo	Hiding things in plain sight through competing recognizable forms
Fractal Pop Camo	Repeated complexity across visual scales
Creaturefield	An entire visual ecosystem of creatures
Pareidolic Camo	Exploiting the brain’s tendency to find familiar forms

My favorite is Semantic Camo.

And there’s an important distinction: traditional camouflage tries to prevent detection. This pattern allows detection but interferes with individuation—separating one recognized object from its surroundings.

That’s a powerful design principle.

For Wild Hawg, imagine a Hawgfield Camo: hundreds of tiny interlocking boars, tusks, lightning bolts, energy symbols, and hidden creatures. At ten feet, it looks like an aggressive, beautiful camouflage. At ten inches, it becomes an entire world people can explore.

You could even use deliberate attention gradients: the large yellow Pikachu shapes serve as dominant focal points while the small Pokémon generate complexity underneath.

The deeper pattern is that the same visual field can carry different information at different resolutions. A texture from far away, a collection up close, and an interactive discovery experience when examined.

That’s more than camouflage. It’s resolution-dependent storytelling.